{
  "pdf_page": 257,
  "book_page": 227,
  "verified_by": "claude-opus-5",
  "verified_on": "2026-09-06",
  "entries": [
    {
      "entry": 0,
      "root": "Ker 3, Qer, Qor, Qr",
      "verdict": "confirmed",
      "modern_form": "PIE *ker-, *kor-, *kr- (Pokorny 'ker-1'; Walde-Pokorny 'qer-, qor-, qr-'; Watkins/AHD 'ker-2'), an echoic base for hoarse cries and for the birds that make them",
      "note": "The root box is exactly right. Pokorny IEW 567-571 heads the entry 'ker-1, kor-, kr-' with the meaning 'a kind of sound (hoarse shrieking, etc.), *crane', and notes 'anlaut mostly k-, rare ḱ- also with moveable s-: (s)ker-' — which is what licenses the s- of Persian sarfa in node 7. Nourai's page reference POK:567 is the first page of that entry, and the four names in his box are not his invention: Walde-Pokorny I 413ff. print the lemma with q for k ('qer-, qor-, qr-'), and Klein (p.1305, s.v. raven) writes 'I.-E. base *qer-, *qor-, *qr-, imitative of harsh sounds'. Watkins/AHD heads the same material 'ker-2. Echoic root, base of various derivatives indicating loud noises or birds. [Pok. 1. ker- 567.]', so the gloss 'loud noise, noisy birds' is a fair rendering of both. Cross-cutting caveats for the page: (a) Cheung 2007 (*xraus, no. 449) accepts the Indo-Iranian root but says an IE provenance for it 'cannot be established', so the Avestan branch (nodes 1-3, 8-9) hangs on Pokorny's judgement rather than on universal consensus; (b) node 4's glosses are looser than the sources (kahrkatāt- is specifically 'cock', kahrkāsa- specifically 'hen-eater'); (c) the parenthesised Persian words ورتک / وردیج in node 5 are synonyms for the bird, not descendants of Av. kahrka- — Mo'in derives them from Old Indic vartikā- 'quail' (Greek ortyx); (d) node 9 (korūh) is a cultural loan from India, not a phonetic descent. No extraction errors: the JSON reproduces the printed chart faithfully (only the fatha of «کَروه ۱» in node 9 is dropped).",
